      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We are given two conditions for the number of coins, \\( N \\), in the box:\n\n1. When \\( N \\) is divided by 6, there are 4 coins left over. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n2. When \\( N \\) is divided by 5, there are 3 coins left over. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest positive integer \\( N \\) that satisfies both congruences. Let's start by expressing \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[\nN = 6k + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\nNext, we substitute \\( N = 6k + 4 \\) into the second congruence:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSubtracting 4 from both sides, we get:\n\\[\nk \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSince \\(-1\\) is congruent to \\(4\\) modulo 5, we have:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nk = 5m + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } m\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( k = 5m + 4 \\) back into the expression for \\( N \\), we get:\n\\[\nN = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28\n\\]\nTherefore, the smallest positive value of \\( N \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\), giving:\n\\[\nN = 28\n\\]\nNow, we need to determine the remainder when \\( N = 28 \\) is divided by 7:\n\\[\n28 \\div 7 = 4 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of } 0\n\\]\nThus, the number of coins left when \\( N = 28 \\) is equally divided among seven people is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
